WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.370
Two speeches.

2
00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:05.710
One cause: keep the town of
Copperwick's swimming pool open.

3
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.080
In the crowd, one listener
has a single thought:

4
00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:10.900
it costs too much.

5
00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.650
The first speaker rushes past that worry.

6
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.140
Reasons, then more reasons.

7
00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:18.090
The thought bubble doesn't move.

8
00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:23.360
The second speaker does something risky,
and admits the cost is a real problem.

9
00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:26.030
Why might that make the argument stronger?

10
00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:29.690
In this mission, you'll build an
argument as one line of reasoning,

11
00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:31.030
not a list.

12
00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.830
Is it about sounding polite?

13
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.180
Or a way to fill a paragraph
when your reasons run out?

14
00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:40.290
And you'll meet the straw man,
an old trick in arguments.

15
00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:45.720
Your mission: explain why admitting the
other side has a point makes you stronger.
